{
  "en": {
    "description": {
      "tfm2_toon_scribble": {
        "name": "Scribble",
        "skill": "Weave: he draws element dots over his head, up to six: 1 Pencil, 2 Eraser, 3 Paint, 4 Gadget, 5 Page. The order matters. Every athlete here is a pro, so even a beginner clicks fast. Mastery (per athlete, from the games they have played on him: an official match counts 1, a scrim or exhibition 0.5, a win 1.5 times that; the badge at his top right) sets their speed in dots a second (CPS) and how long a recipe they build reliably. Any rank can go for any of the 35 spells, but every dot past their comfort is likely to come out wrong (a Novice going for a 6-dot spell: dot 3 80%, dot 4 86%, dot 5 92%, dot 6 98%; each rank above fumbles less). Novice (bronze, 0-4 games): 2.5 CPS, comfortable with 2 dots, often gets a dot wrong and rarely notices, and fires spells at whatever is around; Apprentice (silver, 5+): 3.25 CPS, 3 dots; Adept (gold, 15+): 4 CPS, prepares an opener on the way to a fight and holds it (a catch for a gank, an area spell for a teamfight, an escape when low); Expert (platinum, 30+): 5 CPS, 4 dots, waits for a slow spell's target to be locked down, chains a stun into the big hits, re-picks his opener when the situation changes and drops a held spell that has no use any more (or when another is clearly better), the higher the rank the quicker; Master (diamond, 60+): 6.5 CPS, 5 dots; Grandmaster (ruby, 100+): 8 CPS, almost never slips, wears the Ruby skin (a gold crown, a ruby sigil); Archmage (the rainbow book, 150+): 9.5 CPS, all 6 dots, never slips, wears the Prism skin (a rainbow halo, orbiting pages); Top 10 (the ten athletes with the most mastery on him, 300+ each; a numbered badge, #1 crowned): 11 CPS at #10 up to 15 at #1, the fastest hands in the game, wear the Legend skin (ink wings, a gold halo, gold lightning). Mastery matters more than the athlete's stats. The whole world learns him together: every cast is scored (what it really did against what it promised) and every Scribble player picks spells with what all games so far have taught (each match uses everything played before it; casts from won games and official matches weigh more).",
        "skill2": "Spell book (recipe, cooldown): 1 Pencil Poke (2s): 30 + 50% AP to one enemy. 2 Smudge (3s): rubs out the target's shields, then 20 + 30% AP. 3 Paint Splat (3s): 25 + 40% AP around the target, slowed 20% for 1s. 4 Squeaky Horn (4s): HONK: 15 + 30% AP and a 0.2s stun around him (interrupts). 5 Paper Cut (2s): a flying page: 40 + 60% AP bleeding over 2s. 1-1 Draw a Door (6s): steps through a door 35000 away (out of trouble, or after a fleeing kill). 1-3 Cutout (7s): a cardboard cutout shield 80 + 60% AP for 3s on him or a teammate under fire. 2-1 Erase Myself (8s): invisible for 1.5s. 3-3 Bucket (6s): heals the most hurt teammate near (or himself) 70 + 50% AP. 3-2 Banana Peel (6s): a peel on the ground for 5s: the first enemy on it slips (0.5s airborne, sliding). 4-3 Rubber Chicken (5s): 40 + 50% AP and a knockback. 4-4 Wind-up Key (8s): +40% move and attack speed for 3s. 5-4 ?! Bubble (8s): a confused bubble: the target can't attack for 0.8s. 4-1-5 Say Cheese (10s): a phone photo: everyone in the cone in front (45000) is stunned 1.2s. 4-4-1 Mallet (9s): a giant mallet up close: 60 + 80% AP, stun 1s. 4-2-3 Anvil (12s): a shadow, then an anvil 0.8s later: 90 + 100% AP and a 1s stun to whoever is still under it. 1-5-2 Rubber Arm (10s): a stretchy glove grabs an enemy up to 90000 away and pulls them in. 2-5-1 Erase Legs (11s): rubs out the target's legs: rooted 1.5s. 4-3-3 Pie (10s): a pie in the face: no skills for 2s, no attacks for 1s. 4-1-1 Present (12s): a gift box stuck to the target, opening 2s later: 120 + 110% AP around them. 3-1-3 Slippery Floor (14s): a waxed floor 40000 wide for 4s: enemies on it -30% speed, teammates +25%. 1-4-2-5 Hole Network (18s): draws a hole and pops out 120000 away (home when chased, or into a fight); teammates by him follow 1s later. 4-1-5-5 Group Photo (16s): a panorama shot: everyone in a wide cone 70000 deep is stunned 1.5s. 3-4-3-4 Brawl (18s): a cartoon dust cloud: the target and anyone by them rooted 2s, 4 hits of 30 + 25% AP. 2-2-5-1 Redraw (16s): erases himself and redraws where he was 3s ago, clearing crowd control. 5-3-4-1 Stamp (20s): a giant rubber stamp: enemies around him stunned 1.2s, teammates there shielded 120 + 80% AP. 1-2-1-2 Drawn Wall (20s): draws a wall 90000 long for 5s (between him and his chasers, or behind a fleeing enemy); nobody gets through. 5-5-3-1-4 Rewind (35s): teammates within 70000 go back to where they were 4s ago with the health they had then (if it was more). 4-2-4-1-3 Piano (30s): a shadow, then a grand piano 1s later: 200 + 150% AP and a 1.5s stun in a big circle. 3-5-2-2-1 Ink Flood (30s): a wave of ink 160000 long: 60 + 50% AP, no attacks for 2s, slowed 40%. 1-3-5-4-2 Chase Scene (28s): +100% move speed for 4s; every enemy he runs into is bonked once (40 + 40% AP, 0.3s stun). 2-4-1-3-5 Laugh Track (35s): canned laughter: enemies within 90000 can't attack or cast for 1.5s, teammates heal 60 + 40% AP. 5-5-1-2-3-4 Page Flip (90s): turns the page: every champion in the top or bottom half swaps sides (top <-> bottom); mid stays. 4-4-4-1-5-2 PAUSE (80s): pauses the replay: every enemy champion is frozen 2s. 3-1-2-4-5-3 Draw a Friend (90s): sketches the strongest fallen teammate back in for 10s at 60% of their stats.",
        "ult": "Invoke: casts the spell the dots spell out (a short cast, faster with mastery), then the dots are used up. A sequence that is no recipe, or one whose tier is still on cooldown, fizzles in a puff of smoke. Cooldowns are per number of dots: a spell puts every spell with the same number of dots on its cooldown (after a 5-dot spell, no other 5-dot spell until it is back), while the other tiers stay free. If the spell he wants has its tier on cooldown he goes for the next best one he can cast, keeping the dots that start it (a Novice notices after 1.5s, an Apprentice 1s, an Adept 0.5s, Expert and up at once)."
      }
    }
  }
}